@h1 Derived content h1@

Some content is computed from the document itself — a word count, a reading
time, findings sorted by strength, a "3 of 7 done" badge. Wit's script bridge,
@@@code <% %> code@@@, post-processes a rendered document through an @code lh
code@ object. This page is a cookbook; the full @code lh code@ API lives in the
Build track.

@aside(class note) 💡 Reach for a script last. If @@@code (each) code@@@ over a data collection can produce the view, prefer that — it is declarative, verifiable, and needs no renderer support. Scripts are for genuinely @strong derived strong@ values. aside@

@h2 The surface to rely on h2@

These members are stable enough to build on:

@ul
@li @code lh.data code@ — the document's data definitions. li@
@li @code lh.query(name) code@ — nodes of a given kind. li@
@li @code lh.sort(name, fn) code@ — reorder a collection. li@
@li @code lh.inject(id, src) code@ — insert rendered content at a marker. li@
@li @code lh.prose().wordCount() code@ — a word count of the prose. li@
@li A node exposes @code params code@ and @code content code@. li@
ul@

@h2 Recipes h2@

@strong Word count and reading time: strong@

@@@pre
<%
  const words = lh.prose().wordCount();
  lh.inject('stats', `${words} words · ${Math.ceil(words / 200)} min read`);
%>
pre@@@

@strong Sort findings by strength, then a "X of Y" badge: strong@

@@@pre
<%
  lh.sort('findings', (a, b) => b.strength - a.strength);
  const done = lh.data.findings.filter(f => f.supported === 'true').length;
  lh.inject('badge', `${done} of ${lh.data.findings.length} supported`);
%>
pre@@@

Note the @strong boolean-as-string quirk strong@: a value typed as a boolean
compares as the string @code 'true' code@ here, so write
@code f.supported === 'true' code@. Other recipes follow the same shape —
group items by an owner field, or build a table of contents from
@code lh.query('h2') code@.

@h2 Honesty about the surface h2@

Some members are @strong renderer-specific or unverified strong@ — check them
against your renderer before relying on them: @code lh.target code@ (per-target
dispatch), @code node.params.injectId code@ and @code backrefsInjectId code@,
and a heading's @code kind code@. Treat these as "verify in your build".

@h2 The injection seam that errors h2@

A script that injects @strong rendered content strong@ must supply balanced
nodes. A half-open node — a bare opener with no matching closer — collides with
the surrounding body's close and raises @code E_MISMATCHED_CLOSE code@ (fixture
17-combinations/script-injects-rendered-content; see
@a |href /docs/guides/composing/| Composing constructs a@).

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Reaching for a script when @@@code (each) code@@@ over data would do. li@
@li Relying on unverified @code lh code@ members without checking your renderer. li@
@li Injecting a half-open node (balance the open and close). li@
ul@

